findings.evidence:
**Cause.** maskInText's order is URL → scheme → element → DSN → JWT. The comment at L230-232 and the reply ('The URL and scheme rules can't run past a `>` the way the DSN rule can, so they keep their place') say only the DSN rule runs past '>'. But URL_USERINFO's password class `[^\s/]{0,256}` (L389) crosses '>' and quotes up to the next '@', the same mechanism as round 6's DSN high.

**probe-url-elem.mjs (out-url-elem.txt).** Each shape is in clear at all five heads (2ec0bba to 4033285):
- `<input type="password" name="pw" value="S" data-api="http://api:8080"><small>help@corp.com</small>` gives `…value="Zq9SeCr3tVal" data-api="http://api:[secret b8c22424]@corp.com</small>`;
- `<input name="api_key" value="S" placeholder="https://host:443"><p>Contact:support@example.com</p>`;
- `<input type="hidden" name="csrf_token" value="S" data-href="https://example.com:8443"><span>admin@example.com</span>`;
- `<add key="DbPassword" value="S" uri="redis://cache:6379"><x>ops@corp</x>`;
- probe-elem's `data-u="https://a:b"><b>c@host` (round 6).

Controls are masked: a space before the '@', a '/' after the port, and no '@'. A URL with a port in an attribute, then minified markup holding an email, is realistic.

This is not a regression. But it is not in README:419's misses (no attribute holds '<' or '>'), and not in the corpus, even as a known miss, although round 6's probe-elem reported it.

**A fix exists.** vfix2/data-diff.mjs moves only the URL rule after the element rule (scheme → element → URL → DSN → JWT). It:
- masks all 5 shapes;
- keeps r6_elem_unquoted_bearer masked, since the scheme rule still runs first;
- passes the PR's data-diff and corpus tests (75/75);
- shows the same fuzz residuals as 4033285 against every head, and nothing new (fzv/).

